/**
\addtogroup optimizers

The `laplace::optimizers` module provides the pieces of unconstrained minimization on the host: the objective concepts (`<laplace/optimizers/objective.hpp>`), the report of a run (`report.hpp`), the stopping rules (`stop_rules.hpp`), two line searches (`line_search.hpp`), the dense curvature solver (`dense_curvature_solver.hpp`) and `minimize()` (`minimize.hpp`), all in the same directory. They are header-only templates that run on the host; none is marked `LAPLACE_HD`. A program that uses `dense_curvature_solver` links `Laplace::dense`. On the standard test problems the line searches accept a step within 13 evaluations along \f$-g\f$ and within one along a Newton direction. Newton's method and the trust region are on \ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region"; BFGS and L-BFGS are on \ref optimizers_quasi_newton "Quasi-Newton methods"; the multistart wrapper is on \ref optimizers_multistart "Multiple starts"; Brent's method and block alternation are on \ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation"; Nelder-Mead and compass search are on \ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search"; the proximal methods are on \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods"; the proximal bundle method is on \ref optimizers_bundle "Proximal bundle method"; the primal-dual method is on \ref optimizers_primal_dual "Primal-dual method".

## Objectives

An objective is any type exposing the members a concept asks for; there is no base class. A point \f$x \in \mathbb{R}^n\f$ is a read-only host View, \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point "optimizer_point". Gradients, directions, and trial points are writable \f$n\f$-entry Views, \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector "optimizer_vector", and a symmetric curvature matrix \f$H\f$ is an \f$n \times n\f$ column-major View, \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix "dense_curvature_matrix".

Three concepts form a refinement ladder. Each member evaluates everything requested in one pass, and derivatives are computed only when asked, so a line search evaluates only `value()` at trial points:
- \ref laplace::optimizers::value_objective "value_objective" requires `double value(x)` returning \f$f(x)\f$.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::gradient_objective "gradient_objective" adds `double value_gradient(x, g)`, returning \f$f(x)\f$ and writing \f$\nabla f(x)\f$ (a subgradient where \f$f\f$ is not differentiable) into `g`.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_objective "curvature_objective" adds member type `curvature_type` and `double value_gradient_curvature(x, g, H)`, returning \f$f(x)\f$ and writing `g` and symmetric \f$H(x)\f$. Whether \f$H\f$ is the observed Hessian or an expected information is the objective's choice.

A non-finite value marks \f$x\f$ as a refused trial point, never an error. A non-finite start value (or gradient, when used) gives `start_not_admissible`.

The concept \ref laplace::optimizers::composite_objective "composite_objective" refines `gradient_objective` for \f$f = f_{\mathrm{s}} + r\f$. Here `value()` and `value_gradient()` evaluate \f$f_{\mathrm{s}}\f$ alone, and \f$r(x) = \sum_b r_b(x_b)\f$ is separable over blocks \f$b\f$. It requires `nonsmooth_value(x)` returning \f$r(x)\f$, `nonsmooth_block_count()`, `nonsmooth_prox(v, t, z)` writing block by block \f$z = \operatorname{prox}_{t r}(v) = \arg\min_z \{ r(z) + \lVert z - v \rVert^2 / (2t) \}\f$ for \f$t > 0\f$, and `weak_convexity_constant(b)` returning \f$\rho_b \ge 0\f$ such that \f$r_b + \rho_b \lVert x_b \rVert^2 / 2\f$ is convex (\f$0\f$ when convex). The proximal map is exact only when \f$t \rho_b < 1\f$; see \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods" for the methods and penalty adapter.

The table lists what each optimizer requires, and also serves as the list of what `minimize()` accepts. See \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_objective "curvature_objective" for a complete example.

| Optimizer | Objective concept | Page |
|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::optimizers::newton_optimizer "newton_optimizer" | `curvature_objective` | \ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::trust_region_optimizer "trust_region_optimizer" | `curvature_objective` with `curvature_type` equal to `dense_curvature_matrix` | \ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::bfgs_optimizer "bfgs_optimizer", \ref laplace::optimizers::lbfgs_optimizer "lbfgs_optimizer" | `gradient_objective` | \ref optimizers_quasi_newton "Quasi-Newton methods" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::proximal_gradient_optimizer "proximal_gradient_optimizer" | `composite_objective` | \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::proximal_newton_optimizer "proximal_newton_optimizer" | `composite_objective` and `curvature_objective` with `dense_curvature_matrix` | \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::bundle_optimizer "bundle_optimizer" | `gradient_objective` (a subgradient is enough) | \ref optimizers_bundle "Proximal bundle method" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::primal_dual_optimizer "primal_dual_optimizer" | \ref laplace::optimizers::linear_map_composite_objective "linear_map_composite_objective" | \ref optimizers_primal_dual "Primal-dual method" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::brent_optimizer "brent_optimizer" | `value_objective`, one coordinate | \ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::block_coordinate_optimizer "block_coordinate_optimizer" | `value_objective`, plus what each block's optimizer needs | \ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::nelder_mead_optimizer "nelder_mead_optimizer", \ref laplace::optimizers::compass_optimizer "compass_optimizer" | `value_objective` | \ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search" |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::multistart_optimizer "multistart_optimizer" | What its inner optimizer requires | \ref optimizers_multistart "Multiple starts" |

## Report and status

Every run returns an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_report "optimizer_report" recording why it stopped, where, and at what cost. Its `status` is an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_status "optimizer_status". Two values are successes, and \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_succeeded "optimizer_succeeded()" is `true` for these alone: `converged` (a stationarity rule held at its tolerance) and `converged_at_resolution` (the measure fell below the objective's resolution, about \f$\varepsilon |f|\f$ with \f$\varepsilon = 2^{-52}\f$). The six non-successes are `stopped_on_change`, `stalled` (step below rounding), `iteration_budget`, `evaluation_budget`, `line_search_failed`, and `start_not_admissible` (also the default before a run). Each method's page states when it returns each status. For methods that factorize curvature (Newton, trust region, proximal Newton), `final_factorization` holds a \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_factorization "curvature_factorization" (`positive_definite`, `not_positive_definite`, or `non_finite`); otherwise it stays `not_evaluated`.

Evaluation counters increment per call: `value()` adds \f$1\f$ to `value_evaluations`, `value_gradient()` adds \f$1\f$ to `value_evaluations` and `gradient_evaluations`, and `value_gradient_curvature()` adds \f$1\f$ to all three counters including `curvature_evaluations`. `elapsed_seconds` uses `std::chrono::steady_clock`.

In the objective's units, `decrement` is the decrease predicted by the next step: \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top H^{-1} g\f$ with curvature, or \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top B g\f$ when the next direction is \f$d = -B g\f$ with inverse-curvature model \f$B\f$. It is never \f$|g^\top \Delta x|\f$ for the step \f$\Delta x\f$ just taken, which is near zero after any near-exact line search. Methods with another stationarity measure store it in `decrement`; others leave it quiet NaN. In the units of \f$x\f$, `search_size` holds Brent's bracket half-width, the Nelder-Mead simplex diameter \f$\max_{1 \le j \le n} \lVert v_j - v_0 \rVert_2\f$, or the compass poll step, and is quiet NaN with derivatives.

In proximal Newton, `damped_coordinates` counts inner updates whose diagonal curvature \f$H_{jj}\f$ was replaced (penalized with \f$H_{jj} \le \rho_j\f$ or unpenalized with \f$H_{jj} \le 0\f$). `decrement_from_prox_mapping` is `true` when it falls back to the proximal gradient mapping measure because \f$H\f$ is finite and its free-coordinate restriction has no Cholesky factor (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods"). Both are \f$0\f$ and `false` elsewhere. `multistart_optimizer` (\ref optimizers_multistart "Multiple starts") sums `iterations`, evaluation counts, and `elapsed_seconds` over all starts. Every optimizer has an optional \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_trace "optimizer_trace" member `trace` called per accepted iteration (per sweep in block alternation) with an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_trace_point "optimizer_trace_point" whose Views are valid during the call only. See `optimizer_report` for its fields, defaults, and example.

## Stopping rules

A stopping rule models \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_rule "stop_rule" with `static constexpr unsigned needs` and `stop_verdict verdict(const stop_rule_input&) const`. There is no tolerance beside a rule: the rule is the tolerance. `needs` combines \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_gradient "stop_needs_gradient" (\f$1\f$), \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_decrement "stop_needs_decrement" (\f$2\f$), \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_previous "stop_needs_previous" (\f$4\f$), \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_prox_mapping "stop_needs_prox_mapping" (\f$8\f$), \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_search_size "stop_needs_search_size" (\f$16\f$), and \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_primal_dual "stop_needs_primal_dual" (\f$32\f$); value and iteration are always provided. `stop_rule_for<R, provided>` holds when `(R::needs & ~provided) == 0`, rejecting incompatible rules at compile time.

Verdicts in \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_verdict "stop_verdict" increase from `none` through `stopped_on_change` and `converged_at_resolution` to `converged`. Each call receives a \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_rule_input "stop_rule_input", where previous fields require `has_previous`, unprovided gradients are empty, and unprovided scalar measures are quiet NaN (giving `none`). Stationarity rules may declare convergence, whereas change rules return `stopped_on_change` (never a success), since small changes also occur far from a minimum; `report.decrement` lets the caller judge. The decrement, prox-mapping, and primal-dual rules act in the objective's units independent of \f$n\f$; for example, `decrement_stop_rule{1e-14}` with decrement \f$10^{-12}\f$ where \f$\varepsilon |f| = 2.2 \times 10^{-10}\f$ returns `converged_at_resolution`. `search_size_stop_rule` acts in the units of \f$x\f$ with no resolution test.

| Rule | Needs | Verdict | Defaults |
|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::optimizers::decrement_stop_rule "decrement_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_decrement` | `converged` if \f$\delta \le \tau\f$; else `converged_at_resolution` if \f$\delta \le m\,\varepsilon\,\lvert f\rvert \f$; else `none` | `tolerance` \f$10^{-6}\f$, `resolution_multiple` \f$1\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::prox_mapping_stop_rule "prox_mapping_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_prox_mapping` | Same tests on `prox_mapping` | `tolerance` \f$10^{-6}\f$, `resolution_multiple` \f$1\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::primal_dual_stop_rule "primal_dual_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_primal_dual` | Same tests on `primal_dual` | `tolerance` \f$10^{-6}\f$, `resolution_multiple` \f$1\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::gradient_stop_rule "gradient_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_gradient` | `converged` if \f$\sigma \max_i \lvert g_i\rvert \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \lvert f\rvert \f$; `none` if empty or NaN | `absolute_tolerance` \f$10^{-6}\f$, `relative_tolerance` \f$0\f$, `scale` \f$1\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::search_size_stop_rule "search_size_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_search_size` | `converged` if `search_size` \f$\le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \max_i \lvert x_i\rvert \f$ | `absolute_tolerance` \f$10^{-8}\f$, `relative_tolerance` \f$0\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::objective_change_stop_rule "objective_change_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_previous` | `stopped_on_change` if `has_previous` and \f$\lvert f - f_{\mathrm{prev}}\rvert \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \lvert f_{\mathrm{prev}}\rvert \f$ | `absolute_tolerance` \f$10^{-12}\f$, `relative_tolerance` \f$0\f$ |
| \ref laplace::optimizers::parameter_change_stop_rule "parameter_change_stop_rule" | `stop_needs_previous` | `stopped_on_change` if `has_previous`, no NaN, and \f$\max_i \lvert x_i - x_{\mathrm{prev},i}\rvert \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \max_i \lvert x_{\mathrm{prev},i}\rvert \f$ | `absolute_tolerance` \f$10^{-10}\f$, `relative_tolerance` \f$0\f$ |

\ref laplace::optimizers::any_of "any_of(...)" builds an \ref laplace::optimizers::any_of_stop_rule "any_of_stop_rule" taking the strongest verdict (`none` when empty), while \ref laplace::optimizers::all_of "all_of(...)" builds an \ref laplace::optimizers::all_of_stop_rule "all_of_stop_rule" taking the weakest verdict across at least one rule (so combining convergence with a change rule yields `stopped_on_change`). Both combine `needs` by bitwise union; see `any_of_stop_rule` and `decrement_stop_rule` for examples.

The table lists what each method passes to its rule. Block alternation (\ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation") runs each block's own rule and reports the summed decrements and maximum search size; `multistart_optimizer` (\ref optimizers_multistart "Multiple starts") uses its inner optimizer's rule.

| Method | Measure given to the rule | Field | Default rule | Provides |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Newton, trust region | Newton decrement \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top H^{-1} g\f$ where \f$H\f$ has a Cholesky factor, quiet NaN elsewhere | `decrement` | `decrement_stop_rule` | gradient, decrement, previous |
| BFGS, L-BFGS | \f$\frac{1}{2}\max(\lvert g^\top d\rvert , \gamma_{\max} g^\top g)\f$, \f$d\f$ next direction (\ref optimizers_quasi_newton "Quasi-Newton methods") | `decrement` | `decrement_stop_rule` | gradient, decrement, previous |
| Proximal gradient | \f$\frac{1}{2}\max(t, \gamma_{\max})\lVert G_t\rVert^2\f$, proximal gradient mapping measure (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods") | `prox_mapping` (also in `decrement`) | `prox_mapping_stop_rule` | prox mapping, previous |
| Proximal Newton | Proximal Newton decrement \f$\frac{1}{2} d^\top H d\f$ with its fallback (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods") | `decrement` | `decrement_stop_rule` | decrement, previous |
| Proximal bundle | Model's predicted decrease at a large proximity (\ref optimizers_bundle "Proximal bundle method") | `decrement` | `decrement_stop_rule` | decrement, previous |
| Primal-dual | Primal-dual measure \f$V\f$ (\ref optimizers_primal_dual "Primal-dual method") | `primal_dual` (also in `decrement`) | `primal_dual_stop_rule` | primal-dual, previous |
| Brent | Bracket half-width (\ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation") | `search_size` | `search_size_stop_rule` | search size, previous |
| Nelder-Mead | Simplex diameter from best vertex (\ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search") | `search_size` | `search_size_stop_rule` | search size, previous |
| Compass search | Poll step (\ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search") | `search_size` | `search_size_stop_rule` | search size, previous |

Quasi-Newton methods (\ref optimizers_quasi_newton "Quasi-Newton methods") confirm convergence on the next iteration, keeping the pending verdict if the confirming search returns `step_below_rounding`, `below_resolution`, `not_descent_direction`, or `evaluation_budget`; `stopped_on_change` stops at once. Newton and trust region (\ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region") check the rule only at accepted points. Proximal gradient (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods") resets momentum and retries from the iterate if convergence held at a momentum point. Nelder-Mead and compass search (\ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search") accept convergence only when `value_spread_tolerance` holds (otherwise `stalled`). Everywhere, `stopped_on_change` becomes `optimizer_status::stopped_on_change`.

## Line searches

Given \f$x\f$, \f$f(x)\f$, \f$g\f$, direction \f$d\f$, and initial step \f$s_0 > 0\f$, a line search studies \f$\phi(s) = f(x + s d)\f$ with slope \f$\phi'(0) = g^\top d\f$. \ref laplace::optimizers::armijo_line_search "armijo_line_search::search()" needs a `value_objective` and writes `x_trial`; \ref laplace::optimizers::wolfe_line_search "wolfe_line_search::search()" needs a `gradient_objective` and writes `x_trial` and `g_trial`. Both are `const` and stateless. Sufficient decrease requires \f$\phi(s) - \phi(0) \le c_1 s \phi'(0)\f$, and strong curvature requires \f$|\phi'(s)| \le c_2 |\phi'(0)|\f$ with \f$\phi'(s) = \nabla f(x + s d)^\top d\f$. The difference form prevents rounding \f$\phi(0) + c_1 s \phi'(0)\f$ to \f$\phi(0)\f$, which on a constant objective accepted a step of \f$10^{-12}\f$.

`armijo_line_search` backtracks by safeguarded interpolation (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996, Algorithm A6.3.1), evaluating values only, with defaults `sufficient_decrease` \f$c_1 = 10^{-4}\f$, `min_shrink` \f$\beta_{\min} = 0.1\f$, `max_shrink` \f$\beta_{\max} = 0.5\f$, `max_evaluations` \f$60\f$, and `resolution` \f$0\f$ (no resolution test). At a refused step \f$s\f$ with \f$r_1 = \phi(s) - \phi(0) - \phi'(0) s\f$, the first backtrack uses \f$s_{\mathrm{new}} = -\phi'(0) s^2 / (2 r_1)\f$. Later backtracks use the cubic through \f$s\f$ and the previous refused step \f$s_{\mathrm{p}}\f$ (with \f$r_2 = \phi(s_{\mathrm{p}}) - \phi(0) - \phi'(0) s_{\mathrm{p}}\f$):
\f[
a = \frac{r_1 / s^2 - r_2 / s_{\mathrm{p}}^2}{s - s_{\mathrm{p}}}, \qquad
b = \frac{-s_{\mathrm{p}} r_1 / s^2 + s\, r_2 / s_{\mathrm{p}}^2}{s - s_{\mathrm{p}}}.
\f]
Its minimizer is \f$(-b + \sqrt{b^2 - 3 a \phi'(0)})/(3a)\f$ for \f$b \le 0\f$ and \f$-\phi'(0)/(b + \sqrt{b^2 - 3 a \phi'(0)})\f$ for \f$b > 0\f$, \f$-\phi'(0)/(2b)\f$ for \f$a = 0\f$, and \f$\beta_{\max} s\f$ when \f$b^2 - 3 a \phi'(0) < 0\f$. The step is clamped to \f$[\beta_{\min} s,\, \beta_{\max} s]\f$ (NaN becomes \f$\beta_{\max} s\f$). After a non-finite trial, the step becomes \f$\beta_{\max} s\f$ and interpolation restarts from the quadratic.

`wolfe_line_search` enforces both conditions (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithms 3.5 and 3.6; Moré and Thuente, 1994), evaluating value and gradient at every trial, with defaults `sufficient_decrease` \f$c_1 = 10^{-4}\f$, `curvature` \f$c_2 = 0.9\f$, `max_evaluations` \f$30\f$, and `resolution` \f$0\f$. Bracketing keeps \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ (last trial with sufficient decrease and \f$\phi' < 0\f$, initially \f$0\f$). It zooms on \f$[s_{\mathrm{lo}}, s]\f$ if sufficient decrease fails or \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}} > 0\f$ and \f$\phi(s) \ge \phi(s_{\mathrm{lo}})\f$. Otherwise it accepts if strong curvature holds, zooms on \f$[s, s_{\mathrm{lo}}]\f$ if \f$\phi'(s) \ge 0\f$, or extrapolates by the cubic minimizer in \f$[s + 1.1(s - s_{\mathrm{lo}}),\, s + 4(s - s_{\mathrm{lo}})]\f$ (upper end if none), kept halfway below any refused limit; a refused trial halves toward \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$. Zoom works on a bracket whose ends are \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$, the end with sufficient decrease and the lowest value seen, and \f$s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$. For two steps \f$p\f$ and \f$q\f$ with \f$w = q - p\f$, the minimizer of the quadratic through \f$\phi(p)\f$, \f$\phi'(p)\f$, \f$\phi(q)\f$ and the minimizer of the cubic through \f$\phi(p)\f$, \f$\phi'(p)\f$, \f$\phi(q)\f$, \f$\phi'(q)\f$ (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, eq. 3.59) are
\f[
p - \frac{\phi'(p)\, w^2}{2(\phi(q) - \phi(p) - \phi'(p) w)}, \qquad
q - w\,\frac{\phi'(q) + \eta_2 - \eta_1}{\phi'(q) - \phi'(p) + 2 \eta_2},
\f]
where \f$\eta_1 = \phi'(p) + \phi'(q) - 3(\phi(p) - \phi(q))/(p - q)\f$ and \f$\eta_2 = \operatorname{sign}(w)\sqrt{\eta_1^2 - \phi'(p)\phi'(q)}\f$; the cubic has no minimizer when \f$\eta_1^2 < \phi'(p)\phi'(q)\f$. The same cubic, through \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ and \f$s\f$, is the one used by bracketing. Zoom takes \f$p = s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ and \f$q = s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ and picks the cubic minimizer unless \f$\phi(s_{\mathrm{hi}}) > \phi(s_{\mathrm{lo}})\f$, in which case it picks the cubic if closer to \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ than the quadratic and their mean otherwise (quadratic alone if no cubic), or the midpoint if \f$s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ was refused or no interpolant exists. Trials stay a tenth of the bracket from each end, and a bracket narrower than \f$4\varepsilon \max(s_{\mathrm{lo}}, s_{\mathrm{hi}})\f$ fails with `step_below_rounding`.

Both searches return a \ref laplace::optimizers::line_search_result "line_search_result" with an \ref laplace::optimizers::line_search_outcome "line_search_outcome": `accepted`, `not_descent_direction`, `below_resolution`, `step_below_rounding`, or `evaluation_budget`. Newton defaults to `armijo_line_search`; BFGS and L-BFGS default to `wolfe_line_search` and accept `armijo_line_search` (adding one gradient at the accepted point). They map `step_below_rounding` (BFGS and L-BFGS also `below_resolution`) to `stalled` and other failures to `line_search_failed`; proximal methods use their own backtracking (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods"). The tests (`tests/optimizers/test_optimizers.cpp`) check Armijo within 20 evaluations on Rosenbrock, Wood, and a \f$\kappa = 10^8\f$ quadratic, Wolfe within 10 evaluations from \f$10^{-4}\f$, refused points on \f$x - \log x\f$, and `step_below_rounding` for direction \f$-10^{-300}\f$. See `armijo_line_search` and `wolfe_line_search` for examples.

The table gives evaluations measured with gcc on one thread from the standard starts with initial step \f$1\f$ (`docs/numerics.md`). Quadratics have \f$n = 10\f$ and condition \f$\kappa_2\f$, and the last row uses \f$-H(x_0)^{-1} g\f$ where \f$H(x_0)\f$ is positive definite.

| Problem | Direction | Armijo evaluations | Wolfe evaluations |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rosenbrock (\f$n = 2\f$ and \f$100\f$) | \f$-g\f$ | 5 | 5 |
| Powell singular | \f$-g\f$ | 6 | 4 |
| Beale | \f$-g\f$ | 3 | 3 |
| Brown badly scaled | \f$-g\f$ | 2 | 2 |
| Helical valley | \f$-g\f$ | 4 | 4 |
| Wood | \f$-g\f$ | 10 | 6 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^2\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 3 | 3 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^6\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 7 | 7 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^{12}\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 13 | 13 |
| Positive-definite start | \f$-H(x_0)^{-1} g\f$ | 1 | 1 |

## Dense curvature solver

The host class \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_solver "dense_curvature_solver" allocates three buffers (two \f$n \times n\f$ matrices and an \f$n\f$-entry right-hand side) for dimension `size()` equal to \f$n\f$; a program that uses it links `Laplace::dense`. `factorize(H)` is `factorize_shifted(H, 0)`. `factorize_shifted(H, lambda)` reads the lower triangle of \f$H\f$ without modifying it, computes \f$H + \lambda I = L L^\top\f$ via \ref dense "the dense module", throws `std::invalid_argument` on dimension mismatch, and stores `last_factorization()` (`not_evaluated` initially). After `positive_definite`, `newton_direction(g, d)` solves \f$(H + \lambda I) d = -g\f$ in two triangular solves and returns `true`; otherwise it returns `false` and leaves `d` untouched.

`condition_estimate()` returns \f$\kappa_1(A) = \lVert A \rVert_1 \lVert A^{-1} \rVert_1\f$ for \f$A = H + \lambda I\f$, or \f$+\infty\f$ when not `positive_definite`. \f$\lVert A \rVert_1\f$ is exact, while \f$\lVert A^{-1} \rVert_1\f$ uses Higham's estimate (Higham, 1988, Algorithm 4.1) refining Hager (1984): up to five sign-vector solves plus one solve with \f$x_i = (-1)^i (1 + i / (n - 1))\f$ at \f$O(n^2)\f$ each. Every norm is \f$\lVert A^{-1} x \rVert_1\f$ with \f$\lVert x \rVert_1 = 1\f$, so the estimate never exceeds \f$\kappa_1\f$ beyond rounding. Measured with gcc on one thread (`docs/numerics.md`), the ratio to exact \f$\kappa_1\f$ lies in \f$[0.609, 1.0001]\f$ for \f$n\f$ from \f$5\f$ to \f$300\f$ and \f$\kappa\f$ from \f$10^2\f$ to \f$10^{13}\f$ (excess over \f$1\f$ from rounding at \f$\kappa = 10^{13}\f$). It costs \f$1.21\f$ ms at \f$n = 1000\f$ against \f$16.0\f$ ms for Cholesky and \f$82.4\f$ ms for the inverse by \f$n\f$ solves. The \f$n = 3\f$ test (`tests/optimizers/test_optimizers.cpp`) checks both solves to relative tolerance \f$10^{-14}\f$, an estimate in \f$[1, 10]\f$, and `non_finite` on an infinite entry. It also checks indefinite `not_positive_definite` (`newton_direction()` `false`, infinite estimate) and shift \f$\lambda = 2\f$ leaving `H` untouched.

\ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_solver_for "curvature_solver_for\<H\>::type" maps a curvature matrix type to its solver (its one specialization maps `dense_curvature_matrix` to `dense_curvature_solver`). The solver is used by Newton, trust region (\ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region"), and proximal Newton (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods").

## Running an optimizer

An optimizer carries its settings as public members and provides `optimizer_report run(Objective& objective, const optimizer_point& x0) const` constrained to its objective concept. \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_for "optimizer_for\<Optimizer, Objective\>" holds when that call returns `optimizer_report`, and \ref laplace::optimizers::minimize "minimize(optimizer, objective, x0)" forwards directly to `optimizer.run(objective, x0)` (taking `objective` by non-const reference and `optimizer` by const reference). An objective lacking the required capability or a stopping rule needing unavailable quantities fails to compile. `x0` is unchanged, the result lies in `report.point`, and a maximum of \f$f\f$ is found by minimizing \f$-f\f$. See the table in section "Objectives" above for accepted optimizers, and `minimize()` for an example.

